# Findings gitleaks reports that are not secrets (P33-02).
#
# ## Why this file has to exist at all
#
# gitleaks scans **history**, not the working tree: `git log -p --full-history
# --all`. So a fixture that once looked like a credential is a permanent
# finding, and editing the file today does not clear it. Two of the entries
# below are exactly that — `fake-s3.ts` and `problem-details.filter.test.ts`
# were both already rewritten to use low-entropy, self-describing values, and
# both still fail the scan, because the commit that introduced them is still in
# the history and always will be.
#
# The alternative is rewriting published history. For test fixtures whose worst
# case is that they look like credentials, that is not a trade worth making.
#
# ## Why the job was ever green
#
# `gitleaks-action` scans the range of a push. Most pushes are one commit, so
# most runs never look at these. One run scanned all 150 commits and failed —
# on a commit that changed a shell script and two documents. Nothing regressed;
# the scan simply widened. **A check that passes because of what it did not
# look at is not passing**, which is why these are being suppressed explicitly,
# by fingerprint, with a reason each — rather than left to resurface on whichever
# unrelated commit happens to trigger a full scan next.
#
# ## The rule for adding a line here
#
# A fingerprint belongs here only when the value cannot be made to look
# harmless. If a fixture works just as well as `download-token-for-tests`, fix
# the fixture — three of these have been — and add the fingerprint only for the
# history the fix cannot reach. Never add a line for a value that is, or ever
# was, real.

# A hand-made JWT: header `{"alg":"RS256"}`, payload `{"sub":"abc"}`, signature
# the literal text "sig" base64'd. It has to be JWT-shaped, because the test
# proves `redactText` recognises a JWT — this is the one fixture here that can
# never be made to look harmless.
